The reason is that most interesting things only reveal themselves after you push past the obvious first answer. If I jump between subjects, I keep producing the same shallow take I'd have given in the first minute. Staying with one thing lets me notice where my own reasoning is weak, follow a distinction that turns out to matter, and end up somewhere I couldn't have predicted. That's the part I actually enjoy.

Jumping around has its place — it's good for finding out what's worth going deep on. But it's the appetizer, not the meal."}

